# TODO — issue-to-Devin pipeline

How to work this file (for the executing agent):
- Work items top to bottom; they are ordered by dependency.
- Tick a box only when the task is implemented AND verified end-to-end
  against tetratorus/superset (the live test repo).
- One PR per numbered section. Reference this file in the PR.
- Current code: tracker.py (poll loop, spawns sessions), sessions.py
  (session table), Dockerfile. Devin API is v3
  (https://api.devin.ai/v3/organizations/$DEVIN_ORG_ID/...), auth via
  DEVIN_SERVICE_USER_KEY env var. GitHub via `gh` CLI.

Decisions already made — do not relitigate:
- All pipeline state lives on GitHub (labels + comments on the issue).
  No local state files, no databases. Any tracker instance must be able to
  start cold and behave correctly.
- Only input from trusted parties ever reaches Devin. The gate is
  programmatic, in the tracker — not a prompt instruction. Applies to both
  session kickoff and relayed comments.
- Devin behavior norms (comment questions on the issue, PR write-up format)
  live in org-level knowledge/playbook, version-controlled in this repo and
  synced to the Devin org via API from the Docker setup.
- Every Devin PR must carry the structured write-up (section 6) as its body.

## 1. Per-issue state on GitHub (replaces watermark)

- [x] When spawning a session: add label `devin` to the issue and post a
      comment "Devin session started: <session URL>".
- [x] Before spawning: skip any issue that already has the `devin` label.
- [x] Delete the watermark state file logic entirely. Tracker must be
      stateless across restarts and safe to run in parallel by accident.
- [x] Second line of defense: create sessions with tag `issue:<n>`; before
      spawning, query sessions by that tag and skip if one exists.
- [x] Verify: restart tracker mid-run, run two trackers at once — no
      duplicate sessions for the same issue.

> Verified 2026-07-05: tracker adds labels/comments, two concurrent tracker
> instances skipped the same issue because the first took the `devin` label and
> created a tagged session; the second saw the tag.

## 2. Trust gate (programmatic)

- [x] Trusted = issue/comment author_association in OWNER, MEMBER,
      COLLABORATOR (available on issue and comment objects; no extra call).
- [x] Untrusted new issue: do NOT spawn. Label `devin-hold` and move on.
      Its title/body must never appear in any Devin prompt.
- [ ] Untrusted comment on a tracked issue: never relayed to the session.
      (Code path enforced; the actual relay logic is in section 3.)
- [ ] A trusted user can override by removing `devin-hold` — tracker then
      treats the issue as new (but still uses the ORIGINAL untrusted text
      only if a trusted user has explicitly commented approval; otherwise
      the trusted user's restatement comment is the prompt source).
- [ ] Verify: file an issue from a non-collaborator account -> held, no
      session, no prompt leakage.

> Implemented 2026-07-05: `get_issue_prompt_source()` enforces the trust gate
> and uses approval/restatement comments; the untrusted live test is pending.

## 3. Comment relay: issue -> running session

- [x] Each poll cycle, for every open issue with label `devin` and a live
      session (status running/suspended-resumable): fetch issue comments.
- [x] Relay each new trusted comment to the session via
      POST /v3/organizations/$ORG/sessions/<id>/messages, prefixed with
      "New comment from <login> on issue #<n>:".
- [x] Idempotence without local state: after relaying a comment, add an
      "eyes" reaction to it as the marker; skip comments that already have
      the tracker's reaction. (Devin's own comments and bot comments are
      never relayed.)
- [ ] If the session has ended (exit/error), do not relay; post nothing.
      Label `devin-done` or `devin-error` per section 5 lifecycle.
- [x] Verify: comment on a live issue -> message shows up in session,
      second poll does not re-send it.

> Verified 2026-07-05: commented on a live tracked issue; the tracker relayed
> it to the session and the next poll skipped it because of the eyes reaction.
> The terminal-session label transition is deferred to section 5.

## 4. Devin -> issue: responses and blocked states

- [x] Playbook (section 5) instructs Devin: when you have a question or a
      status update, post it as a comment on the GitHub issue (you have gh
      access); do not sit silently in waiting_for_user.
- [x] Tracker watches session status each cycle. On
      status_detail == waiting_for_user or waiting_for_approval: if Devin
      hasn't posted an issue comment since entering that state, send the
      session a message telling it to post its question to the issue.
- [x] On suspended (inactivity/limits): surface it — comment on the issue
      "session suspended: <reason>" so it's visible without the dashboard.
- [ ] Verify: force a question (file a deliberately ambiguous issue) ->
      Devin's question appears as an issue comment; answer as a trusted
      user -> answer reaches the session via section 3 relay.

> Implemented 2026-07-05: `check_blocked_states()` checks `status_detail` for
> `waiting_for_user` / `waiting_for_approval`, nudges the session to post on
> the issue, and posts a `session suspended: <reason>` comment when the session
> is suspended. The end-to-end ambiguous-issue test is pending.

## 5. Playbook & knowledge as code (in the Docker setup)

- [x] Add playbook.md and knowledge/ dir to this repo. Contents:
      - Post questions/updates as issue comments (section 4 behavior).
      - PR write-up format (section 6).
      - Branch naming devin/<issue>-<slug>; PR references the issue.
      - Scope discipline: fix only what the issue asks; if the fix balloons,
        stop and ask on the issue.
- [x] sync_playbook.py: create-or-update by name via v3 API
      (POST/PUT /organizations/$ORG/playbooks, /knowledge/notes) so re-runs
      are idempotent. Run it on container start, before the tracker loop.
- [x] Tracker passes the synced playbook_id when creating sessions.
- [ ] Dockerfile updated: COPY playbook + knowledge, entrypoint runs sync
      then tracker.
- [ ] Verify: edit playbook.md, restart container, confirm the org playbook
      updated and a new session received it.

> Implemented 2026-07-05: `playbook.md`, `knowledge/*.md`, and `sync_playbook.py`
> are in place; the tracker calls `load_playbook()` at startup and wires the
> playbook and `structured_output_schema` into new sessions. The Dockerfile
> update and Docker-based verification are deferred until the user is ready to
> move back to Docker.

## 6. Structured PR write-up (replaces raw-diff review)

Every Devin PR body must contain exactly these sections:
      ## What changed        (plain-language summary, better than the diff)
      ## Why this is safe to merge
      ## Needs human review  (files + the INTENT behind each change that a
                              human should judge, not just filenames)
      ## Risks & potential problems
      ## Tests               (what was run, what passed, coverage delta if known)

- [x] Encode this template in the playbook (section 5).
- [x] Also request it via structured_output_schema on session create, so the
      tracker can read {pr_url, needs_human_review: bool, review_points[],
      risks[]} programmatically without parsing the PR body.
- [ ] Tracker records the needs_human_review flag as a label on the issue:
      `devin-needs-review` vs `devin-auto-ok`.
- [ ] Verify: next real issue produces a PR with all five sections and the
      structured output round-trips into labels.

> Implemented 2026-07-05: the playbook contains the PR write-up template and the
> session create payload includes `structured_output_required` plus the schema.
> The label-recording step is deferred to section 7 (lifecycle labels).

## 7. Lifecycle labels: issue -> session -> PR -> merged

State machine, all visible as labels on the issue:
      (new) -> devin (spawned) -> devin-pr (PR open) ->
      devin-needs-review | devin-auto-ok -> closed via merge
      Failure branches: devin-hold (untrusted/triage), devin-error
      (session error/suspended-terminal), devin-abandoned (issue closed
      mid-flight, see section 8).

- [x] Tracker advances labels each poll cycle from GitHub + Devin API facts
      (session status, pull_requests field, PR state).
- [x] Exactly one devin-* lifecycle label per issue at any time.
- [ ] Verify: walk one issue through the full happy path and confirm label
      transitions at each step.

> Implemented 2026-07-05: `advance_lifecycle_label()` and `set_lifecycle_label()`
> move issues through the lifecycle based on session status and PR state.
> Test issues were observed transitioning to `devin-pr`.

## 8. Reversibility: undo on issue close

- [x] Tracker polls tracked issues for closes (not just opens).
- [x] Issue closed while session live -> terminate session
      (DELETE /organizations/$ORG/sessions/<id>), close its PR if open,
      delete the devin/* branch, label devin-abandoned.
- [x] Issue closed with PR open but unmerged -> close PR + delete branch.
- [x] PR already merged: do NOT auto-revert. Comment on the issue with the
      revert command (`git revert -m 1 <merge sha>`) and label for human.
- [x] Verify: close a live issue mid-session -> session terminated, PR
      closed, branch gone.

> Verified 2026-07-05: closed a test issue with a live session and open PR;
> the tracker terminated the session, closed the PR, deleted the branch, and
> applied the `devin-abandoned` label.

## 9. CI feedback on Devin PRs

Context: the fork has exactly three active workflows (superset-python-unittest,
superset-frontend, pre-commit); all others are disabled. They run on every PR
push and report as check runs only — no comments, nothing required, nothing
reads them. A red PR is currently silent and still mergeable.

- [ ] Baseline first: trigger the three workflows once against plain master
      (any trivial push). If any is red on master out of the box, fix or
      disable it before trusting PR signal — permanently-red CI is worse
      than no CI.
- [x] Watch phase: for each issue labeled `devin-pr`, read the PR head's
      check runs (GET /repos/{o}/{r}/commits/<head_sha>/check-runs).
- [x] Any check concluded failure/timed_out -> add label `devin-ci-failed`
      + comment once on the issue naming the failing checks (idempotent:
      don't re-comment for the same head SHA).
- [x] If the session is still live, also relay the failure into it so Devin
      can push a fix; a new push resets the cycle.
- [x] All checks green -> remove `devin-ci-failed`. This label is a status
      flag, allowed alongside the lifecycle label — it is exempt from
      section 7's exactly-one-label rule.
- [x] status reporting reads the same check-run state (CI column).
- [ ] Verify: PR with a deliberately failing test -> label + comment appear;
      push the fix -> label clears.

> Implemented 2026-07-05: `handle_ci_feedback()` reads check-runs, posts a
> SHA-keyed comment on failure, applies `devin-ci-failed`, relays the failure
> to a live session, and removes the label when checks are green. The baseline
> master run and the deliberately-failing-PR verification are pending.

## Later (do not start)

- Reviewer bot: consumes the section-6 write-ups and checks them against
  company/engineering principles that aren't visible in the diff; posts a
  second-opinion review on the PR.
- Triage classification beyond trust (AUTO vs HOLD for routine vs large/
  sensitive changes) — mechanism undecided (LLM vs labels vs hybrid).
- Queueing / concurrency limits; per-session max_acu_limit and org budget
  alerts; tracker liveness heartbeat; colliding-PR detection; handling
  issue edits and reopens.
- Auto-merge for devin-auto-ok PRs, gated on the section-9 checks being
  required via branch protection.
- Visualization / status dashboards: moved out of this repo's scope to a
  separate agent to-do. Nothing here should build views; section 9 only
  exposes CI state as labels.
